-# szhou-library
+# szhou-library — FIT5032 Week 11
 
-This template should help get you started developing with Vue 3 in Vite.
+Existing Vue library project, prepared for Cloudflare Pages deployment.
 
-## Recommended IDE Setup
+## Run locally
 
-[VS Code](https://code.visualstudio.com/) + [Vue (Official)](https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=Vue.volar) (and disable Vetur).
+Use Node.js 24.12 or newer (or the supported Node.js 22 version in package.json).
 
-## Recommended Browser Setup
+```sh
+cd szhou-library
+npm ci
+cp .env.example .env.local
+# Set VITE_OPENWEATHER_API_KEY in .env.local to your OpenWeather key.
+npm run dev
+```
 
-- Chromium-based browsers (Chrome, Edge, Brave, etc.):
-  - [Vue.js devtools](https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/vuejs-devtools/nhdogjmejiglipccpnnnanhbledajbpd)
-  - [Turn on Custom Object Formatter in Chrome DevTools](http://bit.ly/object-formatters)
-- Firefox:
-  - [Vue.js devtools](https://addons.mozilla.org/en-US/firefox/addon/vue-js-devtools/)
-  - [Turn on Custom Object Formatter in Firefox DevTools](https://fxdx.dev/firefox-devtools-custom-object-formatters/)
+Open `/WeatherCheck`. Enter `Melbourne, AU` and click **Get Weather**.
+**Current Location** is optional and requires location permission and HTTPS
+(or localhost). Denying permission does not prevent city search.
 
-## Customize configuration
+The weather endpoint and weather icons use HTTPS. The key is injected at build
+time; changing it requires a rebuild. Vite's `VITE_` variables are visible in
+the browser bundle, so this configuration does not make the weather key secret.
 
-See [Vite Configuration Reference](https://vite.dev/config/).
+## Task 11.1a — Deploy from Git
 
-## Project Setup
+In Cloudflare **Workers & Pages**, create a **Pages** project and connect
+`DarrenChouCN/FIT5032`. Use these build settings:
 
-```sh
-npm install
-```
+| Setting                    | Value                                             |
+| -------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |
+| Production branch          | `main` (or the branch containing these changes)   |
+| Root directory             | `szhou-library`                                   |
+| Framework preset           | Vue / Vite                                        |
+| Build command              | `npm run build`                                   |
+| Build output directory     | `dist`                                            |
+| Environment variable       | `NODE_VERSION=24.19.0`                            |
+| Build environment variable | `VITE_OPENWEATHER_API_KEY=<your OpenWeather key>` |
 
-### Compile and Hot-Reload for Development
+Set the weather key for Production and Preview as needed before building.
+Cloudflare Pages handles Vue Router history fallback automatically when there
+is no top-level `404.html`. Verify a direct visit and refresh of `/WeatherCheck`.
 
-```sh
-npm run dev
-```
+Firebase's backend lives in `firebase-functions/`; `firebase.json` points to
+that folder. It remains deployed separately using Firebase. This avoids
+Cloudflare treating the Firebase backend as Pages Functions. Do not use
+`npm start` (the old Express example) as the Pages build command.
 
-### Compile and Minify for Production
+## Task 11.1b — Direct deployment / C3
+
+The lecture also requests a separate C3-created Vue Pages project. From outside
+this repository, follow its flow (use your initials in the name):
 
 ```sh
-npm run build
+npm create cloudflare@latest -- szhou-library-cli --framework=vue --platform=pages
 ```
 
-### Lint with [ESLint](https://eslint.org/)
+Complete the prompts, log in to Cloudflare when asked, and deploy. Keep this
+separate from the Git-connected project; do not replace the existing library
+source with a new scaffold. C3 setup and deployment must actually be run to
+claim that part of the practical.
+
+For subsequent CLI deployments of this existing library project:
 
 ```sh
-npm run lint
+cd szhou-library
+npx wrangler login
+npm run deploy
 ```
+
+The deploy script builds and uploads `dist` to the `szhou-library` Pages project.
+If your Pages project has another name, update the script's `--project-name`
+and `wrangler.jsonc` before deploying. Set `.env.local` before the local build.
+
+## Task 11.2 — Check deployed weather
+
+1. Visit `https://<your-project>.pages.dev/WeatherCheck`.
+2. Enter `Melbourne, AU`, click **Get Weather**, and wait for live results.
+3. Confirm city/country, temperature in Celsius, description, and icon appear.
+4. Refresh this direct URL and check city search again.
+5. Optionally verify Current Location with browser permission enabled.
+
+Missing/invalid keys, unknown cities, request limits, timeouts, and location
+permission failures have visible error messages. Do not use mocked weather as
+submission evidence.
+
+## Screenshot checklist — capture after deployment and verification
+
+| Evidence                  | What must be visible                                                                                                                                                                                      |
+| ------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| 11.1 Git deployment       | Cloudflare dashboard with project name, repository/branch or commit, successful deployment, and deployed URL (as in the lecture's success screenshot).                                                    |
+| 11.1 C3/direct deployment | Cloudflare project/success page identifying the separate CLI project and deployed URL; retain the C3 terminal success output as supporting evidence.                                                      |
+| 11.2 working Get Weather  | The actual deployed weather page with the browser address bar showing its `pages.dev/WeatherCheck` URL, Get Weather navigation/button, and a successful city/country, temperature, description, and icon. |
+
+Use clear, readable screenshots. A page-only automation screenshot normally
+omits browser chrome and does not satisfy the address-bar requirement. Capture
+the browser window for Task 11.2. Include the working deployed project link in
+the final submission. The lecture asks for one combined PDF for Weeks 11 and
+12, each item on a new page with a heading. The Week 12 Actions screenshot and
+TTFB/hotlink answers shown in the rubric are separate work.
+
